<p>The Straits Times reports that hundreds of buildings in Kyiv are without heat after power disruptions linked to Russian attacks on Ukraine’s energy system, calling it Ukraine’s worst energy crisis of the nearly four-year-old war. A spell of cold weather has made the situation harder. RT, citing a Financial Times interview with Kyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko, says the capital is facing large-scale outages and a growing dispute over who should run the response. Pressure on infrastructure isn’t limited to Ukraine. The Kyiv Independent reports that Ukrainian missile and drone attacks hit power stations in several Russian regions, and officials in Bryansk said the regional capital suffered major losses of electricity and heat supply.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>FT remark highlighted by RT: Klitschko told the Financial Times that “the question of the future of our country – whether we will survive as an independent country or not is still open,” as cited by RT.</li><li>Who controls the response: RT says Klitschko accused Zelensky of infringing on municipal authority by appointing military-civilian administrations. Zelensky, RT adds, has argued Kyiv is falling behind other cities in responding to outages.</li><li>Political context noted by RT: RT says Zelensky continues to exercise presidential powers under martial law even though his term expired in 2024.</li><li>Russia regions hit: The Kyiv Independent reports widespread outages in several Russian oblasts after a large-scale Ukrainian missile and drone attack on power stations.</li><li>Bryansk disruption: Bryansk Governor Alexander Bogomaz said the regional capital, Bryansk, lost most of its electricity and heat supply after the strikes, according to the Kyiv Independent.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Kyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko: In remarks RT attributes to an FT interview, he linked the blackouts to wider uncertainty about Ukraine’s future and criticized steps he says weaken municipal authority, including the use of military-civilian administrations. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/632564-mayor-klitschko-kiev-brink-catastrophe/'>rt.com</a>)</li><li>Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky (as summarized by RT): RT says Zelensky argued Kyiv was lagging behind other Ukrainian cities in its response to power outages. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/632564-mayor-klitschko-kiev-brink-catastrophe/'>rt.com</a>)</li><li>Russian government/military (as described by RT): RT says Russia argues its strikes on Ukraine’s power grid aim to undercut weapons production and are retaliation for Ukrainian attacks on Russian civilian infrastructure. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/632564-mayor-klitschko-kiev-brink-catastrophe/'>rt.com</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/hundreds-of-buildings-without-heat-in-ukraine-capital-kyiv'>Hundreds of buildings without heat in Ukraine capital Kyiv</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/632564-mayor-klitschko-kiev-brink-catastrophe/'>Kiev on the brink of catastrophe – mayor</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://kyivindependent.com/ukrainian-drone-attack-targets-multiple-russian-oblasts-bryansk-left-largely-without-electricity/'>Widespread power outages are reported by authorities in several oblasts in Russia, including Bryansk Oblast and Belgorod Oblast, following a large-scale Ukrainian missile and drone attack on Russian power stations. The Governor of Bryansk Oblast Alexander Bogomaz confirms the regional capital Bryansk has lost most of its electricity and heat supply. (The Kyiv Independent)</a> - kyivindependent.com</li></ul>